          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong functorial factorization
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            that factors any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composite
            of two morphisms, in a way that depends
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functorially
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+squares"} commutative squares
          %p
            Functorial factorizations play a prominent role in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            theory. On the one hand, many constructions there do rely on the factorizations into (acyclic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            and (acyclic)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
            to be functorial, while, on the other hand, via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
            many examples of model categories do in fact carry a functorial factorization. (As a result, some authors include functorial factorization in the axioms of a model category right away.)
          %p
            Functorial factorizations also play an important role as an ingredient in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+weak+factorization+systems"} algebraic weak factorization systems
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#concrete_explicit Concrete explicit
          .un_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong functorial factorization
              on a category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a way of assigning to any arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              a pair of composable arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi L
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_L, f_R
              such that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi R
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi L
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f_R \circ f_L
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+square"} commuting square
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi h
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp; \overset{h}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\\
                    {}^{\mathllap{f}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{g}}
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \overset{k}{\longrightarrow}
                    }
            %p
              a morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi h
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(h,k)
              completing their factorizations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi R
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f_R \circ f_L
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi R
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = g_R \circ g_L
              to a further
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mi h
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
